<body>

<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Active Satellites in Earth Orbit</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">2026 Catalog Analysis — 14,875 Spacecraft Across All Orbital Regimes</div>
    <div class="meta">Data sourced from CelesTrak / NORAD TLE catalog &middot; Analysis conducted May 2026 &middot; Epoch range: 2026-02-26 to 2026-03-30</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- ===== KPI SUMMARY ===== -->
<div class="kpi-grid">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="value">14,875</div>
    <div class="label">Total Active Satellites</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card" style="border-top-color:var(--emerald-500)">
    <div class="value">86.5%</div>
    <div class="label">LEO Share</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card" style="border-top-color:var(--amber-500)">
    <div class="value">10,083</div>
    <div class="label">Starlink Constellation</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card" style="border-top-color:var(--violet-500)">
    <div class="value">53.2&deg;</div>
    <div class="label">Median Inclination</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card" style="border-top-color:var(--rose-500)">
    <div class="value">90.5%</div>
    <div class="label">Launched Since 2020</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ===== SECTION: OVERVIEW ===== -->
<section>
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">01</span> Catalog Overview &amp; Key Figures</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>This report analyzes the <strong>14,875 active satellites</strong> tracked in the NORAD / CelesTrak TLE catalog as of late March 2026. The dataset captures the operational state of Earth's orbital population across all regimes — from the International Space Station in Low Earth Orbit to geostationary communications platforms at 35,786 km altitude.</p>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>Headline:</strong> Only <strong>1,414 satellites</strong> (9.5%) currently active were launched before 2020. The other <strong>13,461</strong> (90.5%) were deployed in the past six years — a growth curve without precedent in the history of spaceflight.
    </div>

    <div class="stat-row">
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="num">14.33</div><div class="desc">Mean MEAN_MOTION (rev/day)</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="num">15.27</div><div class="desc">Median MEAN_MOTION (rev/day)</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="num">60.0&deg;</div><div class="desc">Mean Inclination</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="num">0.0023</div><div class="desc">Mean Eccentricity</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="num">2026-02-26</div><div class="desc">Earliest EPOCH in catalog</div></div>
      <div class="stat-item"><div class="num">2026-03-30</div><div class="desc">Latest EPOCH in catalog</div></div>
    </div>

    <p>The median MEAN_MOTION of 15.27 rev/day reflects a population dominated by satellites orbiting at ~500–550 km altitude — the preferred shell for Starlink and many other LEO constellations. The mean eccentricity of 0.0023 confirms that the vast majority of active satellites occupy near-circular orbits. The EPOCH data spans approximately one month (Feb 26 – Mar 30, 2026), indicating a fresh and internally consistent dataset.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION: ORBIT REGIME ===== -->
<section>
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">02</span> Orbital Regime Classification</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>Satellites are classified into four orbital regimes using <strong>MEAN_MOTION</strong> thresholds (revolutions per day), following the standard TLE-based methodology:</p>
    <table class="data-table" style="max-width:540px; margin:12px auto;">
      <tr><th>Regime</th><th>MEAN_MOTION Criterion</th><th class="num-col">Count</th><th class="num-col">Share</th></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-leo">LEO</span></td><td>MM &gt; 14 rev/day</td><td class="num-col"><strong>12,860</strong></td><td class="num-col">86.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-meo">MEO</span></td><td>1.1 &lt; MM ≤ 14 rev/day</td><td class="num-col">1,383</td><td class="num-col">9.3%</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-geo">GEO</span></td><td>0.9 ≤ MM ≤ 1.1 rev/day</td><td class="num-col">596</td><td class="num-col">4.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="badge badge-heo">HEO</span></td><td>High eccentricity (e &gt; 0.1) + distinctive MM</td><td class="num-col">36</td><td class="num-col">0.2%</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="dual-chart">
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="regimeChart"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="regimeNoStarlinkChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>Starlink's impact on regime statistics is decisive.</strong> LEO appears to dominate with 86.5% share, but when Starlink is excluded, the remaining 4,792 satellites show a far more balanced distribution: LEO 57.9% (2,777), MEO 28.9% (1,383), GEO 12.4% (596), and HEO 0.8% (36). Starlink alone accounts for <strong>78.4% of all LEO objects</strong> in the catalog.
    </div>

    <h3>LEO Sub-Shell Breakdown (by MEAN_MOTION)</h3>
    <table class="data-table" style="max-width:500px;">
      <tr><th>Sub-Shell</th><th>MEAN_MOTION Range</th><th class="num-col">Count</th></tr>
      <tr><td>VLEO</td><td>MM &gt; 16 rev/day</td><td class="num-col">79</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mid-LEO (Starlink-dominant)</td><td>15 &lt; MM ≤ 16 rev/day</td><td class="num-col"><strong>11,014</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Upper LEO</td><td>14 &lt; MM ≤ 15 rev/day</td><td class="num-col">1,767</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:8px; font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--slate-500);">The Mid-LEO band (MM 15–16) corresponds to ~450–600 km altitude and contains 85.6% of all LEO satellites, overwhelmingly driven by Starlink deployments in the 53&deg; and 43&deg; inclination shells.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION: CONSTELLATION ===== -->
<section>
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">03</span> Major Constellation &amp; Operator Breakdown</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p>Constellation affiliation is inferred from the <strong>OBJECT_NAME</strong> prefix. A single operator — SpaceX — dominates the catalog:</p>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <canvas id="constellationChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>Starlink (SpaceX) operates 10,083 satellites — 67.8% of all active spacecraft in orbit.</strong> The next-largest operator, OneWeb, fields only 651 satellites (4.4%). The top-10 constellations together account for 78.1% of the total catalog, with the remaining ~3,200 satellites distributed across hundreds of smaller operators, government missions, and scientific payloads.
    </div>

    <h3>Top 10 Operators / Constellations</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Constellation / Operator</th><th class="num-col">Active Satellites</th><th class="num-col">Share of Total</th></tr>
      <tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Starlink</strong> (SpaceX)</td><td class="num-col">10,083</td><td class="num-col">67.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>OneWeb (Eutelsat)</td><td class="num-col">651</td><td class="num-col">4.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Project Kuiper (Amazon)</td><td class="num-col">210</td><td class="num-col">1.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Yaogan (China)</td><td class="num-col">165</td><td class="num-col">1.1%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>Hulianwang Digui (China)</td><td class="num-col">154</td><td class="num-col">1.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6</td><td>Cosmos (Russia)</td><td class="num-col">126</td><td class="num-col">0.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>Flock (Planet Labs)</td><td class="num-col">119</td><td class="num-col">0.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>Qianfan (China)</td><td class="num-col">108</td><td class="num-col">0.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>Iridium</td><td class="num-col">80</td><td class="num-col">0.5%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>Lemur (Spire Global)</td><td class="num-col">76</td><td class="num-col">0.5%</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p style="margin-top:12px; font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--slate-500);">China's emerging megaconstellations (Yaogan, Hulianwang Digui, Qianfan, Jilin, and Geesat) collectively account for 604 satellites — an early indicator of a multi-operator race in LEO broadband and Earth observation. Amazon's Project Kuiper has 210 satellites active, representing the early deployment phase of its planned 3,236-satellite constellation.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===== SECTION: LAUNCH TRENDS ===== -->
<section>
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">04</span> Launch Year Trends</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>Launch year</strong> is extracted from the first four characters of <strong>OBJECT_ID</strong> (e.g., "2024-001A" → 2024). This represents the year the satellite was placed in orbit, not the year of catalog entry. Because the dataset includes only <em>currently active</em> satellites, the chart below reflects survival-weighted trends — older launches are underrepresented due to natural decay and deorbiting.</p>

    <div class="callout-danger">
      <strong>The acceleration is extraordinary.</strong> Nearly half of all active satellites — <strong>4,329 (29.1%)</strong> — were launched in 2025 alone. More satellites were launched and kept active in 2025 than existed in the entire active catalog as recently as 2022 (then ~4,800).
    </div>

    <div class="chart-wrap">
      <canvas id="launchTrendChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="dual-chart" style="margin-top:12px;">
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="cumulativeChart"></canvas>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <canvas id="launchShareChart"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Launch Year Summary</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
      <tr><th>Period</th><th class="num-col">Active Satellites</th><th class="num-col">Share</th><th>Significance</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Pre-2000</td><td class="num-col">86</td><td class="num-col">0.6%</td><td>Legacy missions; most have decayed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000–2009</td><td class="num-col">260</td><td class="num-col">1.7%</td><td>Pre-constellation era</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010–2019</td><td class="num-col">1,068</td><td class="num-col">7.2%</td><td>Early smallsat/cubesat growth</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>2020–2026</strong></td><td class="num-col"><strong>13,461</strong></td><td class="num-col"><strong>90.5%</strong></td><td><strong>Megaconstellation era</strong></td></tr>
    </table>

    <p style="margin-top:12px;">The top five launch years by currently active count are all post-2020: <strong>2025</strong> (4,329), <strong>2024</strong> (2,477), <strong>2023</strong> (2,328), <strong>2022</strong> (1,804), and <strong>2021</strong> (1,063). The 2026 count (962 through March) is on track to exceed 2024 levels if the deployment cadence holds.</p>

    <p>Even excluding Starlink, the post-2020 acceleration remains striking: <strong>3,395 non-Starlink satellites launched since 2020</strong> are still active — representing 70.8% of the non-Starlink catalog. The trend is broad-based, not solely a Starlink phenomenon.</p>
