    <p>The dominant peaks are at <strong>~53&deg;</strong> (Starlink Group 1–7 shells) and <strong>~97&deg;</strong> (Sun-synchronous orbits used by Earth observation satellites). A secondary cluster near <strong>40&deg;</strong> corresponds to Starlink's lower-inclination shells. The median inclination shifts dramatically from 53.2&deg; to 87.9&deg; when Starlink is excluded, revealing a non-Starlink population that leans heavily toward polar and SSO missions.</p>

    <h3 style="margin-top:24px;">5.2 Eccentricity Distribution</h3>
    <p>Eccentricity (e) quantifies orbital shape: 0 = perfect circle, approaching 1 = extreme ellipse. The overwhelming majority of active satellites occupy near-circular orbits.</p>

