<section>
  <h2 class="section-title"><span class="num">06</span> Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>1. The Starlink Effect Is Total — and Growing.</strong> One operator fields 67.8% of all active satellites. Starlink's 10,083 spacecraft skew every aggregate statistic: LEO share, median inclination, mean eccentricity, and the launch growth curve. Any analysis of the orbital environment that does not segment by operator risks being a Starlink analysis in disguise.
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>2. The Megaconstellation Era Has Reshaped Space Access.</strong> With 90.5% of active satellites launched since 2020, the orbital population has turned over almost completely in six years. The active catalog in early 2020 (~1,900 satellites) has been multiplied <strong>7.8&times;</strong> by sustained high-cadence launches, primarily from SpaceX but increasingly from OneWeb, Amazon (Project Kuiper), and Chinese state-backed constellations.
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>3. LEO Congestion Is Concentrated at Specific Altitudes.</strong> The Mid-LEO band (MM 15–16, ~450–600 km) hosts 11,014 satellites — 74.1% of the entire catalog. This altitude range is the preferred deployment zone for broadband megaconstellations. Conjunction risk, debris mitigation, and spectrum coordination are all concentrated in this narrow shell.
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box">
      <strong>4. The Non-Starlink Population Tells a Different Story.</strong> Excluding Starlink, the remaining 4,792 satellites distribute more evenly: 57.9% LEO, 28.9% MEO, 12.4% GEO. The median inclination rises to 87.9&deg;, reflecting the dominance of sun-synchronous polar orbits in Earth observation, weather, and government missions. Multiple non-Starlink constellations (OneWeb, Kuiper, China's Hulianwang Digui/Qianfan) are in active deployment, suggesting the operator-concentration problem may ease — even as total population continues to climb.
    </div>

    <div class="callout-danger" style="margin-top:20px;">
      <strong>5. Orbital Sustainability Is Now a First-Order Concern.</strong> The current 14,875 active satellites represent a ~7.8&times; increase over 2020 levels. With multiple operators planning constellations of 3,000–13,000+ satellites, the active catalog could exceed <strong>30,000–50,000</strong> objects within this decade. Collision avoidance maneuvers, post-mission disposal compliance, and debris mitigation enforcement will define whether this growth trajectory is sustainable or carries systemic risk.
